{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of room proof reuse at head 57fb198836c1b9a083c1cc855a8b2e4e2b0a17f7, tree e6323467, for request 7c0e8181 and review request 957f91ef. Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.\n\nThe head adds eight commits to main 5c6ea9bb and changes 17 paths. I read the implementation note first, then the production diff in `packages/replay/src/verify.ts`, `packages/cli/src/commands.ts`, `packages/cli/src/verification.ts` and `scripts/demo/rehearse.ts`, the contract and README text, the tests and the sealed evidence. A second, independent read of the same source checked cache keys, expected heads, contradictions, failed folds, depth, shared text, accounting, close and the standalone path. I confirmed each of its findings in the code before using it here.\n\nThe design follows ruling 8e0c4ff6, and the room completes under shipped defaults. Two defects in the room's final settlement can report a scope consistent when the room's own rule, or the standalone rule, makes it incomplete or a mismatch.\n\n## Changes requested\n\n1. A later blocked position hides an earlier problem in the same scope.\n   - `#settleRoom` seeds the problem map from `#blocked` (`verify.ts:682`). It adds a missing-text debt only if the scope has no problem yet (`verify.ts:685`). `finishRoom` then overwrites every scope with its blocked position (`verify.ts:721`), and it adds an unwalked ancestry record or an incomplete byte span only to a scope with no problem (`verify.ts:722-723`). Each scope keeps one problem, and that problem starts at the blocked position, not at the earliest problem.\n   - The spread skips every dependent whose edge lies below `problem.from` (`verify.ts:731`).\n   - Scenario:\n     1. Root R uses a fact at entry 10 of scope B.\n     2. Entry 5 of B names a detached text. B's source does not hold it, and no later entry redacts it.\n     3. Settlement reads B on to its head and finds a mismatch at entry 20, which blocks B from 20.\n     4. B's problem starts at 20, and the debt at 5 is dropped. R's edge at 10 lies below 20, so R reports consistent.\n   - Without the mismatch at 20, the same rule marks B from 5 and R incomplete. A standalone `verify` of R reports the mismatch. The same masking applies to an unwalked record or an incomplete byte span below a blocked position.\n   - Fix: for each scope, keep the problem with the smallest `from`. Add a replay case with a debt below a later block, and a control that restores the first-written merge and fails the same assertion.\n\n2. The room settlement recovers missing texts for definitions without capacity.\n   - The standalone `#settle` recovers a debt only when the definition declares capacity (`verify.ts:1215`). Without capacity, only a redacting entry answers a missing text, under section 9.3 of the contract.\n   - In a room run, `#keepInput` records every canonical text body that any scope reads, with no capacity check (`verify.ts:1113-1124`). `#recoverRoomDebts` (`verify.ts:653-661`) then deletes the debt whenever `#recoverableText` accepts it, and for a definition without capacity that holds except after a refused founding.\n   - Scenario: scope B declares no capacity and names a text that its source lacks, and no entry redacts it. Another scope in the same run holds the text. The room reports B and its users consistent. A standalone `verify` reports incomplete. One root is enough.\n   - Ruling 8e0c4ff6 lets detached text that a checked source settles serve another target \"under the same binding\". It does not remove the capacity rule. The new recovery cases use only definitions with capacity.\n   - Fix: apply the same capacity check in `#recoverRoomDebts` as in `#settle`, and add a case with a definition without capacity. If the builder believes that the room rule should differ, the question goes to the planner before the head returns.\n\nThese changes touch production source outside the documents-only rule, so ruling 31962e1f needs one further full gate at the final head. Gates 1 and 2 stay failed, and gate 3 stays a pass for source G.\n\n## Against request 7c0e8181 and rulings 8e0c4ff6, 2a308f5d and ad166a42\n\n1. Reuse. Resolved apart from items 1 and 2.\n   - One owner serves every requested scope. A proof keys on scope, incarnation, kind, sequence and hash. Reuse also requires that the sealed hash match at that position.\n   - Retained bodies enter the cache only as canonical, digest-checked bytes. Each owner holds one bound source and a snapshot of the options.\n   - A cached root re-reads its first page and compares its head and its expected head. The cached-head control shows that check.\n   - A failed fold rebuilds from held bytes before the next reuse.\n   - `finally` closes both owners on every path, and every step checks `#live()`.\n   - Standalone `verify` changes only behind the room flag.\n2. Defaults and accounting. Resolved.\n   - The shipped limits are unchanged. The room's scope limit takes the request budget, as `remaining()` already did at 5c6ea9bb (`verification.ts:73-74`).\n   - The CLI charges a new entry identity before it stores it, and it refuses conflicting bytes or frames for one identity. Every HTTP attempt and response byte is still charged.\n   - The unique-entry control `control-unique-entry-2` fails two ordinary assertions with its check removed, and ruling ad166a42 keeps the first control as inconclusive.\n3. The witness. Resolved.\n   - The native case at D runs one default `verify --all`. It reports all 2,006 scopes consistent, with all 2,001 Inbox Session histories, in 38.754 seconds and 12,076 HTTP attempts.\n   - Gate 3 at G runs the same case again, and it passes in 154 seconds. D to G does not change the proof source.\n   - Row H is the observed prior-head run that ruling 2a308f5d accepts. The note states its override and the byte bound that stopped it.\n4. Existing tests. Resolved for the request's condition. No existing replay test file changed, and the head only adds `room-verification.test.ts` and `room-recovery.test.ts`. The CLI, lane and seal tests outside replay change with the new output shape: `verify.test.ts` only adds assertions, and the room case follows rulings 8e0c4ff6 and 2a308f5d.\n5. The contract and README. Resolved. Section 9.4 and the README say that depth counts from the nearest root and that a room proof has the requested scopes as roots.\n\n## The gates\n\n- Gate 1 at D failed on two seal cases, and it stays failed.\n- Gate 2 at F failed five native cases on the old output shape, and it stays failed.\n- G repaired the output contract in three paths, so a further full gate was needed. Gate 3 at G, f2fb7384 with tree e46bcbe4, passes all five stages. Its receipt has SHA-256 3ffe4fa9, exit 0, and the same head and tree before and after.\n- G to the head changes only the note and the manifest, which the documents-only rule in `docs/testing.md` covers.\n\n## Checks at this head\n\n- The worktree is clean. `git merge-tree --write-tree` onto main 5c6ea9bb gives the head tree e6323467.\n- I reran the owning files once each, with no whole suite:\n  - `room-verification.test.ts`, `room-recovery.test.ts`, `verification.test.ts` and `verify.test.ts`: 22 of 22;\n  - `rehearsal-stops.test.ts`: 3 of 3;\n  - `security-entry-points.test.mjs`: 10 of 10.\n- I read the native room case's retained report and its gate-3 record rather than rerun it.\n- The builder worktree stayed clean after the runs.\n- The manifest has SHA-256 9c5056e4 and 24,352 bytes. It follows ruling 4e199bd8:\n  - all 168 listed files match by size and SHA-256;\n  - `finalization` and `operator-inputs` match their digests, and each SHA256SUMS reruns clean, with no unlisted file;\n  - the root SHA256SUMS, digest d2caa18f, covers all 875 other files, with none unlisted and none writable.\n- check-doc-links on an export of the head: 760 links, 0 broken.\n- check-notes from 5c6ea9bb: 0 warnings.\n- check-commit-messages from 5c6ea9bb: 8 checked, 0 flagged.\n- `git diff --check`: clean.\n- All 17 artifacts at the head are current, and none is stale.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. The union record can drop a text's source. A text learned while the entry under check belongs to another scope has no recorded origin, and `finishRoom` then omits its row.\n2. A rebuild re-checks a scope's whole sealed prefix without charging entries, as the once-per-run rule allows. Only the time limit bounds that work.\n3. The note is hard to read. Many sentences carry three or four claims, and some use the passive (\"is quarantined\", \"are bound\"). The note says that the native room case ran at D, and it can add that gate 3 ran the same case at G.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe room proof stays in the replay library, and the CLI only drives it. Standalone `verify` keeps its semantics, and no state outlives the command. The room settlement now repeats rules that the standalone `#settle` already owns, and item 2 comes from that repetition. Approved, subject to items 1 and 2.\n\n## Security\n\nThe cache binds every proof to its exact source, scope, incarnation, hash and options, and discovery proves nothing. Items 1 and 2 are soundness defects: in each, `dap verify --all` reports a scope consistent when its history owes a text or holds a mismatch. Changes requested.\n\n## Simplification\n\nOne owner replaces 2,001 separate traversals, and the default room completes. The room path adds 558 lines to `verify.ts`. One shared recovery rule for `#settle` and `#recoverRoomDebts` would remove the class of defect in item 2. Approved, with that point.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved, subject to items 1 and 2.\n- Security: changes requested, items 1 and 2.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- The request's checks: reuse, the unchanged defaults, unique-entry accounting, the default room witness, the accepted prior-head control, the unchanged replay tests and gate 3 hold. The evidence verifies under ruling 4e199bd8. Items 1 and 2 need a fix, a case and a control each, and one further full gate.\n\nA head that makes items 1 and 2 can come back for a short review of the diff.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6a646ab2ed24680c55595c83ae58771cf6c0f555","binding":"assigned","head":"57fb198836c1b9a083c1cc855a8b2e4e2b0a17f7","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fd5d561b62c8b2a66831fdde421cbd6c7528abd9\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:310185bf8291b7c7d4a373d845a449b6bc374e9a\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:84572e366c276d0e08ad7c5287eb9dd51db6253b","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}